# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true in its system config, so a fresh
# clone there would check every file out with CRLF endings. Measured
# consequence: `gofmt -l .` then lists every .go file as unformatted and the
# build gate is red before a line of code is written. This file makes the
# working tree LF everywhere, for us and for anyone who clones us.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.go   text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.pem  text eol=lf

# Byte-exact protocol fixtures must never be touched by any line-ending
# policy. A 0x0A rewritten to 0x0D 0x0A inside a frame or an HPACK vector
# reads as "my parser is broken" and costs hours to find.
testdata/**/*.bin -text
testdata/**/*.raw -text

# `text=auto` above would detect these as binary on its own. Saying so is not
# redundant: the cost of a wrong guess is a corrupt file, and this is the one
# file in the repo whose job is to leave nothing to detection.
*.png -text
